# libnma -- the NetworkManager GUI widgets, and the reason gtk3 is packaged.
#
# gnome-control-center 48.4's meson.build 298-306 requires libnma-gtk4
# UNCONDITIONALLY under host_is_linux, with no required:false, and its
# meson_options.txt has no network option at all -- so there is no way to build
# Settings without this, and no way to build this without GTK 3. The chain is:
#   gnome-control-center -> libnma-gtk4 -> libnma -> gtk+-3.0
#
# VERIFIED IN THIS TARBALL rather than taken from the brief: meson.build's
# gtk_dep is a TOP-LEVEL declare_dependency wrapping dependency('gtk+-3.0'),
# outside every conditional. -Dlibnma_gtk4=true does not replace it; it builds
# a SECOND library beside it. So enabling GTK 4 support adds GTK 4, it does not
# remove GTK 3.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env -- versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh. The sha256
# was checked against upstream's own published libnma-1.10.6.sha256sum, not
# only against the bytes that arrived.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/libnma/1.10/libnma-1.10.6.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=53a6fb2b190ad37c5986caed3e98bede7c3c602399ee4f93c8fc054303d76dab
SRC_DIR=libnma-1.10.6

# -Dlibnma_gtk4=true. DEFAULTS TO FALSE, and it is the entire point of the
# package here: without it the build produces libnma (GTK 3) and no
# libnma-gtk4, and gnome-control-center's unconditional dependency does not
# resolve. post-install.sh asserts BOTH libraries, because the GTK 3 one
# building successfully says nothing about whether the GTK 4 one was built at
# all -- and the GTK 3 one is the default, so a lost flag leaves a package that
# looks complete.
#
# -Dgcr=false. gcr is the "advanced certificate chooser", and gcr-3/gcr-4
# belong to another chain and are not packaged. Worth knowing that this loss
# lines up with one already recorded: NetworkManager here is built -Dcrypto=null
# and therefore cannot load or verify a certificate at all, so an 802.1X
# connection with a CA or client certificate cannot be configured whatever
# widget is offered for choosing one. The two are consistent, and both are
# recorded rather than quietly accepted.
#
# -Dmobile_broadband_provider_info=false. Defaults TRUE and is a build-time
# check for a database that is not packaged. It is the APN list the mobile
# broadband wizard prefills from -- and NetworkManager is currently built
# -Dmodem_manager=false, so there is no mobile broadband panel to prefill.
# Revisit with ModemManager, not before.
#
# -Diso_codes=true is the default and is kept: iso-codes IS packaged, and it is
# what translates country names in the mobile and Wi-Fi region pickers. Named
# because it is the one enabled-by-default check in this file that survives.
#
# -Dgtk_doc=false and -Dvapi=false both default TRUE. gtk-doc is not packaged;
# vala is not packaged. Neither is a soft probe -- each is a build-time
# requirement of a documentation or bindings target nothing here consumes.
#
# -Dintrospection=true is the default and is required: gnome-control-center
# reaches these widgets through GObject introspection, so the typelib is not
# optional decoration. Declaring it means declaring gobject-introspection AND
# glib-introspection -- the scanner and the typelibs it reads are different
# packages.
MESON_ARGS="-Dlibnma_gtk4=true -Dgcr=false \
-Dmobile_broadband_provider_info=false -Diso_codes=true \
-Dgtk_doc=false -Dvapi=false -Dintrospection=true"
